//! Plain-language mental models and concrete procedures for the 18 roadmap topics.
pub struct Guide {
    pub analogy: &'static str,
    pub steps: &'static [&'static str],
}
pub const GUIDES:&[Guide]=&[
 Guide {analogy:"Think of an array as numbered lockers: a position takes you straight to one locker. A map is a labeled coat check: a key helps locate its associated value, even when keys are not consecutive numbers.",steps:&["Start with an empty count map and read the input from left to right.","For each value, look up its count. Treat a missing value as zero.","Increase that count by one. Leave every other count unchanged.","After the last value, counts describe the whole input. Compare counts to find the most frequent value; break ties as the question specifies."]},
 Guide {analogy:"Two bookmarks approach each other in a sorted list. The order tells you which bookmark can move without overlooking an answer.",steps:&["Sort the values if the question does not already provide sorted input.","Put left at the first position and right at the last. Keep them distinct.","Compare their sum with the target. A small sum moves left rightward; a large sum moves right leftward.","Stop when the sum matches or the bookmarks meet. Explain which candidates each movement ruled out."]},
 Guide {analogy:"A stack is a pile of plates: the last plate placed on top is the first one you can remove. Nested brackets use that same last-in, first-out rule.",steps:&["Begin with an empty stack of unmatched opening brackets.","Push each opening bracket as you read it.","For a closing bracket, require a matching opening bracket on top, then pop it. An empty or mismatched top means failure.","At the end, accept only if the stack is empty; a remaining opener still needs a closing partner."]},
 Guide {analogy:"Finding a name in an alphabetized directory is easier when each comparison lets you discard half the remaining pages.",steps:&["Check that the input is sorted. Keep low and high around the possible answer range.","Inspect the midpoint. If its value equals the target, return that position.","If the midpoint is smaller, move low past it. If larger, move high before it.","Repeat until the range is empty. Then report that the target is absent."]},
 Guide {analogy:"A moving picture frame shows only a fixed stretch of a long row. Moving it one place hides one value and reveals one new value.",steps:&["Compute the sum of the first window of the required size.","Move one position right: subtract the value leaving and add the value entering.","Compare the updated sum with the best sum seen so far.","Continue until the last complete window. The example uses fixed-size windows; variable windows need an additional validity rule."]},
 Guide {analogy:"A linked list is a chain of notes, each telling you where the next note is. To reverse the chain, change one arrow at a time while keeping the rest reachable.",steps:&["Keep previous empty and current at the head.","Save current's old next link before changing anything.","Point current back toward previous. Move previous to current and current to the saved next node.","When current becomes empty, previous is the new head. The reversed prefix and untouched suffix always account for the original nodes."]},
 Guide {analogy:"A binary search tree is a sequence of signposts: smaller values turn left, larger values turn right. The path depends on the order in which values were inserted.",steps:&["The first inserted value becomes the root.","Compare a new value with the current node: take the left or right branch according to the ordering rule.","Insert at the first empty child position. This lab ignores duplicate keys.","Repeat for the remaining input. Check every subtree, not just immediate children, against its inherited ordering bounds."]},
 Guide {analogy:"A trie is a word-building path. Cat and car share the route c → a, then split. A word-end marker distinguishes car from a longer word beginning with car.",steps:&["Start at the root before the first character.","Follow the edge for the next character, creating that child if necessary.","Continue through every character and mark the last node as a complete word.","For another word, reuse matching prefix edges. Shared paths save repeated prefixes; terminal markers preserve distinct words."]},
 Guide {analogy:"A min heap keeps the smallest priority at the front of the line without sorting everyone else. A parent only promises to be no larger than its children.",steps:&["Append the new value at the next complete-tree position.","While it is smaller than its parent, swap upward until the heap rule holds.","To extract the minimum, remove the root, move the last value to the root, and sift it down through the smaller child.","This visual trace shows the state after each completed insertion or operation, not each individual swap."]},
 Guide {analogy:"Backtracking explores a decision tree like trying routes through a maze: choose, explore, then undo the choice before trying the next route.",steps:&["For the current item, make a choice to include it or exclude it.","Recurse to decide about the next item while retaining the current partial subset.","At the end of the input, record the completed subset.","Return and undo temporary choices so the next branch starts with the correct prefix. There are 2^n include/exclude combinations."]},
 Guide {analogy:"Breadth-first search spreads outward in waves. All vertices one edge away are discovered before vertices two edges away in an unweighted graph.",steps:&["Mark the source discovered, set distance zero and enqueue it.","Remove the oldest queued vertex and inspect its neighbors.","For each undiscovered neighbor, record current distance plus one, mark it discovered and enqueue it.","Continue until the queue is empty. Unreachable vertices remain undiscovered. Marking on discovery avoids duplicate queue work."]},
 Guide {analogy:"Dynamic programming keeps answers to smaller questions so you can reuse them. Stair counting asks how many routes can reach each step.",steps:&["Define dp[i] precisely: the number of ways to reach step i using jumps of one or two.","Establish the base cases for the empty start and the first step.","For each later step, add the ways to arrive from one step below and two steps below.","Return the last entry. When only the final count is needed, two rolling values can replace the full table shown for teaching."]},
 Guide {analogy:"Overlapping appointments can be covered by one longer block. Sorting by start time lets you check the next interval against just the current merged block.",steps:&["Sort intervals by their starting point.","Keep the first interval as the current block.","If the next interval overlaps, extend the current end to the larger end. Otherwise, store the current block and start a new one.","Store the final block. These examples use closed intervals, so touching endpoints merge."]},
 Guide {analogy:"For interval scheduling, finishing earlier leaves the most room for later work. The greedy choice must be justified for this exact objective, not assumed for every optimization problem.",steps:&["Sort candidate intervals by finishing time.","Select the earliest-finishing interval that is compatible with your last selection.","Update the last finish and keep scanning; reject overlapping candidates.","These examples use half-open intervals, so the next job may start exactly when the last one ends. Compare with merging's endpoint convention."]},
 Guide {analogy:"Dijkstra keeps tentative travel costs. With nonnegative edges, the unsettled vertex with the smallest cost is safe to settle next.",steps:&["Set source distance to zero and every other tentative distance to infinity.","Choose the unsettled vertex with the smallest finite distance.","For each outgoing edge, compare its neighbor's old distance with current distance plus edge weight; keep the smaller value.","Settle the current vertex and repeat. The teaching trace scans for the minimum; a heap is a separate implementation choice. Negative weights invalidate this rule."]},
 Guide {analogy:"A grid table records how many paths reach each square. To arrive at a square while moving only right or down, you must come from its left or upper neighbor.",steps:&["Define the count at the start as one if it is unblocked.","Visit cells in row order. Give every blocked cell zero paths.","For every other cell, add the count from above and from the left, treating missing neighbors as zero.","The destination count is the result. The table preserves the smaller subproblems needed for each next cell."]},
 Guide {analogy:"Binary represents a number with on/off switches. Subtracting one flips the lowest on-switch and the zeros after it, giving a useful way to remove one set bit.",steps:&["Begin with a nonnegative integer and a count of zero.","While the value is nonzero, replace it with value AND (value minus one).",
"Each operation clears exactly its lowest set bit; increase the count once.","Stop at zero. The count is the number of original one-bits, and the loop runs once per set bit."]},
 Guide {analogy:"Euclid's algorithm repeatedly removes whole multiples. The common divisors of two integers are unchanged when the larger is replaced by its remainder on division.",steps:&["Start with nonnegative a and b, with at least one nonzero value.","Compute a remainder a mod b while b is nonzero.","Replace the pair with b and the remainder.","When the second number reaches zero, the first is the greatest common divisor. The remainders strictly decrease, so the process terminates."]},
];
